# Machine-specific git config: commit identities, private hosts, per-machine tweaks.
#
# `make link` copies this template to `config/git/config.untracked` on a fresh
# machine, and ONLY if that file doesn't exist yet (an existing one is never
# overwritten). That copy is git-ignored, so anything you put in it — including
# secrets — is NEVER committed. Edit the copy, not this template.
#
# The copy is pulled in by the [include] at the end of ./config and loaded
# AFTER it, so any value set here overrides the tracked config.
#
# Commit email per folder. ./config sets no user.email and has
# user.useConfigOnly = true, so committing fails in any repo that no rule
# below matches. A repo's own `git config user.email` still wins.

# Personal: personal.gitconfig (tracked) holds the personal email.
[includeIf "gitdir:~/development/toomuchdesign/"]
	path = personal.gitconfig
[includeIf "gitdir:~/.dotfiles/"]
	path = personal.gitconfig

# Another email for another folder, Nearform as the example:
#   1. Create the identity file next to this one. Any name ending in
#      .gitconfig.untracked is git-ignored. git can't take the email inline
#      in an includeIf, so every rule points at a file:
#        printf '[user]\n\temail = andrea.carraro@nearform.com\n' > nearform.gitconfig.untracked
#   2. Uncomment the rule below, or copy it for another folder. `path` is
#      relative to this file; the trailing slash on gitdir matches every repo
#      under that folder. Folders that share an email can share the file.
#   3. Check: git -C <repo> config --show-origin user.email
#
# [includeIf "gitdir:~/development/nearform/"]
# 	path = nearform.gitconfig.untracked
